no the government has prioritized  getting the economy restarted  in the first stage of easing the  lockdown and the clock is  certainly ticking for many companies  already on the verge of going bust  to discuss this further we're joined by  the director general of the cbi  den carolyn ferb and dem fairban really  lovely to see you this morning um  how bad is it out there for your members  at the moment i think it is it is really  tough i mean we are  we have seen the furlough working well  so we got 7.5  million jobs currently protected but i  think where businesses are now is  thinking about  how we protect those jobs for the future  and  that is about a cautious careful  restart this week i think actually from  the business perspective  has been a week of glimmers of light at  the end of the tunnel  we have the encouragement at the  beginning of the week for businesses  to begin to get back to work they are  following that putting safety first  protocols  in place uh perspex staggered shifts and  so on  but what we are concerned about is that  the policies need to be coordinated  across transport across schools across  the devolved nations  and if we don't have consensus that will  undermine  confidence it will undermine confidence  amongst workers  amongst amongst the consumer as  consumers and people in general  so how we sought transport how we sought  schools  and how we get much more joined up  thinking across scotland wales northern  ireland  england is potentially a break on this  cautious restart that we now need  as you said glimmers of light for for  some of your members many  members many members many of me perhaps  many of your members  one does wonder though given this week  there has been so much talk about  whether or not the government advice has  been  straightforward whether or not it's been  confusing i i mean what are your members  telling you about the  the detail do they have the granularity  that they need  to be able to get back to work well  interestingly i think  in general the guidance has worked well  um and and it does give a level what  businesses are saying is they want  flexibility within a framework not too  prescriptive because every  business is different but clarity around  what social distancing does need to look  like  in different environments how you do  organize  lifts in in office buildings for example  and that clarity  um has come through and i think  crucially now this was done  with unions and so that is working and i  think what we now want to see  is that same kind of collaborative  approach in transporting  a lot of businesses are phoning me and  saying  actually our big worry is about how we  get our employees to work  safely what happens if they can't and  they are looking to see that kind of  collaboration so  we would like to see uh transport unions  and the government  come to an agreement that isn't where we  are now the same thing on schools we  have  many employees who are dependent on  schools being open particularly in  retail about one and a half million  non-food retail folk uh can't get back  to work unless they have  uh schools primary schools open we need  that same kind of consensus building  in other areas as well a moment ago you  mentioned  the role of transport in the transport  sector  in getting everyone back to work i  wonder what you think  of the quarantine plans as outlined by  the government perhaps  two weeks if you arrive into the united  kingdom in quadranting before you can  get out and about there  that's not necessarily going to be  helping business but then again  we've all been video conferencing all  the way through this one suspects that's  going to be carrying on for quite some  time  i have to say i think aviation getting  getting people flying again  would be an incredibly powerful boost to  our economy we're an island  economy we are service based we have  engineers who are flying in and out  of the country fixing equipment i've  been hearing from businesses in  in aerospace in manufacturing who are  really worried about  uh this potential quarantine so i think  what we would like to see  is an international standard because at  the moment you've got different  countries doing different things  uh and that is is very bad for global  trade as we were hearing  uh earlier um we do want to see a a  a real collaboration with um the  aviation sector to make  flying safe i think it is interesting  also to look at other airports around  the world we see  vienna and hong kong introducing testing  at airports as an  alternative uh to quarantine so you know  the aviation  getting people flying matters far more  than just for the aviation  sector it matters for getting our  economy open and we  do ask the economy the government to  think very carefully  about how this is introduced um so that  it doesn't put the brakes  on our economy in this fragile recovery  there was some fascinating research from  the federation of small businesses that  you may have seen this week  that one in three of uh funds that have  closed  during uh the lockdown fear that they  will  will never reopen what can be done  to help those businesses which so often  are described as the lifeblood  the backbone of our economy and yet you  know we're talking about thousands tens  of thousands of firms  believing that in the not too distant  future the chances of them reopening are  pretty much zero  i i think this is a a a huge question  and because as  i said we have we have seen the furlough  scheme protect seven and a half million  jobs  uh so far but those jobs are not safe  into the future um unless we get the  economy  growing again the furlough system the  government support schemes can't stay  in place forever so i think we need to  restart the economy very carefully and  cautiously it would be very good  to start seeing plans for the future  around infrastructure  investment investment in trading some of  the things that will  encourage businesses to think about how  they can  uh tackle new markets and and get back  on  their feet we may also need to think  about how we support those businesses  particularly smaller ones  coming out of this recession with equity  support  and possibly an additional round of  grants  but the biggest thing now is to continue  in a coordinated way  everyone joined up building trust public  trust  so that those small businesses and  larger businesses can open  safely and we can get back to growth uh  and save those jobs  what is your view and then fair about  the assertion that the government is  more interested in the health of the  economy than the health of the nation  i really think this is a false dichotomy  you cannot have a strong economy without  public safety the worst thing that could  happen would be  another lockdown these these two  objectives go hand in hand and i think  one of the things that we can draw real  encouragement from  is how businesses across the country are  making their workplaces safe i've seen  example after example from from  supermarkets now we have garden centers  manufacturers in fact 60 of the economy  has stayed open  throughout this crisis working in  partnership with our employees and  unions  to stay open we should not be seeing  these two things  in conflict we must build a consensus  going forwards about how we manage the  two together  we will be living with the virus for a  while and we mustn't have these two  uh in conflict they go hand in hand  carolyn ferban always good to talk  do stay well we'll see you again in the  program soon
 

